{"name":"Poster Blue","hex":"#134682","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"poster","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A picture of a celebrity, an event etc., intended to be attached to a wall.","An advertisement to be posted on a pole, wall etc. to advertise something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate with posters."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/poster"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Poster","blocks":[{"text":"A poster is a large sheet, usually made of some type of paper or plastic, that is placed either in a public space to promote something or on a wall as decoration. Typically, posters include both textual and graphic elements, although a poster may be either wholly graphical or wholly text. Posters are designed to be both eye-catching and informative. Posters may be used for many purposes. They are a frequent tool of advertisers, propagandists, protestors, and other groups trying to communicate a message. Posters are also used for reproductions of artwork, particularly famous works, and are generally low-cost compared to the original artwork. The modern poster, as we know it, however, dates back to the 1840s and 1850s when the printing industry perfected colour lithography and made mass production possible."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Vichy-Louis_Tauzin.jpg/330px-Vichy-Louis_Tauzin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poster","wikidata":"Q429785"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Chemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_%281910%29.jpg/500px-Chemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_%281910%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Chemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_%281910%29.jpg/1280px-Chemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_%281910%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Chemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_%281910%29.jpg/500px-Chemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_%281910%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChemin_de_fer_du_Midi_-_Tauzin_Louis_(1910).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Train-PdD.jpg/500px-Train-PdD.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/